  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe current best bound is 0.38085, while the provided solution achieves 0.38086. We aim to improve this by leveraging a multi-resolution coordinate descent approach with asymmetric initial sampling. This will:\n   - Improve local explore by using elliptical coordinate steps\n   - Balance global search across resolutions\n   - Avoid local optima by driving low-bias samples\nKey improvements:\n   - Use sampling that ensures twice as many samples in high-risk autocorrelation zones\n   - Implement directionally-aware coordinate descent steps (focus where non-overlap peaks)\n   - Build progressive expansions toward optimal solution\n   - Use transaction-based search tracking with rollback on failure\nThe approach addresses the target by explicitly driving min-overlap and modeling the auto-correlation penalties with ripple effects.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nProblem: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over h\u2208[0,1] with \u222bh=1.\nRepresentation: h(x) represented by samples over [0,2] with steps dx=2/n. Risk exposure defined as:\n   R_entry(k) = h(x) * (1-h(x+k)) for x,x+k \u2208 active domain\n   With risk-driven gradients computed via:\n   - Rising penalty for regions where base auto-correlation =0\n   - Aggressive near-zero auto-correlation detection\nWe delta-solve for h entries using:\n   dh\u6eff\u8db3 [\u2207_autocorr + g*grad_overlap] * dh + min_max_coeff = 0 \nwith g being step size determined by autocorrelation curvature\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n1. Block initialization: \n   - Use `initial_h_values` if provided\n   - Convert uniform samples to elliptical stress concentration sampling\n   for x in [0,2]:\n      \u03c3 = sigmoidal_transform(x)  # concentration \u03c3(x)\n      p = \u03c3(x) / \u03c3(x)+\u03c3(2-x)    # directional stress\n      Sample density = base_density * p + (1-p)*base_density\n2. Core algorithm:\n   def risky_search(n_points, target_overlap):\n      Initialize h from stress_density(x)\n      Set base_score = compute_c5(h)\n      For k in range(max_attempts):\n        If time_limit exceeded: break\n        Pick critical point x0 where nausea_score(x) highest \n        Compute excitation vector e(x) = [1-autocorr(x,k)] for all k\n        Use conjugate gradient optimization up to learn_dx\n        Project to feasible domain\n        Evaluate new_score = compute_c5(new_h)\n        If improvement: accept(h) else partially revert\n        Combine with lottery selection from all solutions\n3. Progressive expansion:\n   Use bubble sort of solutions by score\n   Keep top N solutions\n   Expand solution space one resolution step at a time (if time)\n   Budget management: Allocate fractions: 50% bench, 30% risk, 20% expand\n4. Solution acceptance:\n   - Only accept if autocorrelation penalty improvement > min_rising_threshold\n   - Track best seen across all resolutions and restarts\n   - Maintain separate population of valid integrals\n\n4. Search choices and use variations:\n   - Primary: stress-density sampling with directional adjust\n   - Secondary: adaptive exploration windowing\n   - Termination condition: max_cycles or time_limit reached\n   - Hyperparameter variations include:\n        opticAlpha=[0.1,0.5]       # Gradient control\n        sampleRatio=[0.8,0.9,0.95] # Autocorrelation sampling density\n        criticalPointsRatio=[0.1]   # Low autocorrelation emphasis\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Principal cost: coordinate-wise gradient evaluations\n   - n_points=700 => cost ~ 700 evaluation per C5 check\n   - Time budget: 1100s => ~250 per attempt\n   - Deadline checks in every iteration cycle\n   - Rollback on gradient explosion: safe steps only\n   - Valid fallback: interpolated parent structure\n   - Objective recheck: always verify final solution with true calculator\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   Final steps:\n   - Validate h[x]\u2208[0,1] for all x\n   - Project integral to exactly n_points/2\n   - Reevaluate C5 with high-precision calculation\n   - Check function evaluations are finite\n   - Return tuple (numpy.array(h_clean), c5_optimized, n_points_pickled)\n\nThe plan completes by searching simultaneously across stress points and resolution levels, with performance optimizations via gradient-based updates and rollbacks. This method independently models the auto-correlation with targeted sampling and achieves solutions with higher min-overlap penalty.",
